From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 08:56:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 35/43] fix(gastown): make polecat submit-and-exit close its
 own step bead (gcp-rz8a)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mol-polecat-work's submit-and-exit ended at `gc runtime drain-ack; exit` without
closing its own step bead. The step bead stayed open and assigned to the session
name, and because the molecule carries gc.session_affinity=require the pool kept
respawning that same named session, whose `gc hook --claim --json` handed it the
already-completed submit-and-exit with no context that the work was done. The
respawn is not just noise: a re-run of the reassign step writes
`--assignee=$REFINERY_TARGET --set-metadata gc.routed_to=""`, which on a bead the
refinery parked with gc.routed_to="human" after refusing to land on an armed
require_merge_approval gate would silently pull that bead back out of the
operator escalation. Both things that prevented it on gcp-s14g were accidental:
the branch-shape gate only failed closed because the branch had already been
deleted, and the prompt-side ALREADY_SUBMITTED guard could not run at all
because $GC_BEAD_ID was empty in that session.

The primary fix is the one the bead and the mayor's direction both prefer: the
step now resolves its own step bead up front (by `gc.step_ref`, scoped to this
session's assignee — never $GC_BEAD_ID, which for a polecat is the convoy) and
closes it before draining, so the step cannot be re-claimed at all. The
auto_push=false halt closes it too, because that path reaches the step's
contracted end; the push-failure paths deliberately do not, because the handoff
never happened and the next session must re-claim and retry.

Three further brakes, each requested by the bead:
- An up-front already-submitted guard bails out before any bead write when all
  three marks of a completed handoff are present (target recorded, branch on
  origin at this commit, bead assigned to somebody who is not this session).
  All three are required together: target alone can be set by a caller to pick
  the base branch, and an unassigned bead whose branch is on origin is the shape
  a witness salvage push leaves behind (gcp-7bo9).
- The refinery handoff refuses to run when gc.routed_to reads "human", leaving
  assignee and routing exactly as the escalation set them and noting the halt on
  the bead. The branch is already pushed by then, so nothing is lost.
- The prompt-side guard (duplicated in the polecat prompt and the
  approval-fallacy fragment) recovers the convoy from the step bead's molecule
  root when $GC_BEAD_ID is not exported, and now keys on positive evidence
  instead of "no longer in_progress for me". That old predicate was doubly
  wrong: a molecule's work bead is never assigned to the polecat session, so
  making the guard runnable without fixing the predicate would have made it
  report already-submitted on work that was never submitted, and drain with the
  branch unpushed.

Validation: new contract tests in gastown/tests/test_gastown_pack_assets.sh
cover the step-bead close (including its ordering before the drain, and the
push-failure paths that must NOT close it), the human-routing guard and its
ordering before the update that clears gc.routed_to, the already-submitted guard
running before the first bead write, and both copies of the prompt guard. Shown
red against the pre-change assets and green after, one asset at a time. Full
gastown shell suite, `go vet/build/test`, golangci-lint and validate_registry
are clean; the 10 python failures in the wider suite reproduce unchanged on HEAD
(GC_BIN leaking into the intake-service tests, bare `bd` strings in two gastown
test scripts, and timeouts in the gascity claim tests) and are filed separately.
